WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.089 --> 00:00:04.569
Speaker 1: I want to start today with an explosive
confrontation that took place during another

00:00:04.569 --> 00:00:07.330
one of these Republican Senate hearings.

00:00:07.330 --> 00:00:14.330
This particular confrontation involved unhinged
Republican Senator Markwayne Mullin as well,

00:00:14.330 --> 00:00:20.720
or with all along side or up against, I guess
you would say, Teamsters President Sean O'Brien.

00:00:20.720 --> 00:00:26.019
Teamsters, of course, a union Republicans
tend not to like unions.

00:00:26.019 --> 00:00:31.230
Republicans love to frame unions as sort of
like mob entities and the guys or gals who

00:00:31.230 --> 00:00:34.450
run unions as mob bosses.

00:00:34.450 --> 00:00:38.100
And we've seen that narrative for a while.

00:00:38.100 --> 00:00:43.260
The big continuing story since Republicans
have taken over control of the House is the

00:00:43.260 --> 00:00:49.010
humiliating face planting and self-destructive
nature of these hearings, the hearings that

00:00:49.010 --> 00:00:50.120
Republicans have organized.

00:00:50.120 --> 00:00:56.609
And this is happening in the House and the
Senate on big and crossover hearings as well,

00:00:56.609 --> 00:01:02.730
on big tech, on weaponization of the justice
system or whatever the case may be.

00:01:02.730 --> 00:01:10.270
And even when it is not Republicans who organized
the hearing, they still find new ways to humiliate

00:01:10.270 --> 00:01:11.270
themselves.

00:01:11.270 --> 00:01:12.840
So let's take a look at this video.

00:01:12.840 --> 00:01:19.430
Bernie Sanders was sort of trying to officiate
this thing.

00:01:19.430 --> 00:01:27.040
And it is the latest brazen attempt by Republicans
to attack labor, which is what they love to

00:01:27.040 --> 00:01:28.040
do.

00:01:28.040 --> 00:01:31.510
Speaker 2: You talk about CEOs that are making
all this money.

00:01:31.510 --> 00:01:33.360
And what do you make, Mr. O'Brien?

00:01:33.360 --> 00:01:35.310
Speaker 3: Well, I'm glad you asked that question.

00:01:35.310 --> 00:01:44.380
Speaker 2: I know what you make because in
2019, your salary was, what is this, 193,000?

00:01:44.380 --> 00:01:46.079
I'm sure you got some pay raises since then.

00:01:46.079 --> 00:01:47.079
Speaker 3: Yeah, well, numbers.

00:01:47.079 --> 00:01:48.079
Speaker 2: Are going to average you.

00:01:48.079 --> 00:01:51.820
Speaker 1: So basically, like the same salary
as Markwayne Mullin gets as a senator.

00:01:51.820 --> 00:01:52.820
That's an interesting thing.

00:01:52.820 --> 00:01:53.820
A little higher.

00:01:53.820 --> 00:01:54.820
Speaker 2: Yes.

00:01:54.820 --> 00:01:55.820
Driver.

00:01:55.820 --> 00:01:56.820
The feeder driver makes 35,000 a year.

00:01:56.820 --> 00:01:58.020
That's what you bring.

00:01:58.020 --> 00:01:59.020
Speaker 3: That's inaccurate.

00:01:59.020 --> 00:02:00.219
That's an inaccurate state.

00:02:00.219 --> 00:02:01.409
Facts right here.

00:02:01.409 --> 00:02:02.409
State facts.

00:02:02.409 --> 00:02:04.320
Speaker 2: That's an average U.P.S.

00:02:04.320 --> 00:02:05.930
feeder driver makes 35,000.

00:02:05.930 --> 00:02:07.500
If you don't know your facts.

00:02:07.500 --> 00:02:08.500
And maybe you.

00:02:08.500 --> 00:02:09.500
Speaker 3: Should.

00:02:09.500 --> 00:02:10.500
I know because I negotiate the car.

00:02:10.500 --> 00:02:12.330
Speaker 2: So I say one thing to you.

00:02:12.330 --> 00:02:13.520
What do you bring for that salary?

00:02:13.520 --> 00:02:14.550
What do I bring?

00:02:14.550 --> 00:02:19.590
What do you what job have you committed or
have you have you started?

00:02:19.590 --> 00:02:21.030
What job have you created?

00:02:21.030 --> 00:02:26.250
One job other than sucking a paycheck out
of some other by somebody else that you want

00:02:26.250 --> 00:02:28.540
to say that you're trying to provide Because
you're so.

00:02:28.540 --> 00:02:34.220
Speaker 1: Remember, the narrative here is
unions are oppressive.

00:02:34.220 --> 00:02:40.810
The people who run them take money in exchange
for nothing other than forcing the workers

00:02:40.810 --> 00:02:42.750
to pay union dues.

00:02:42.750 --> 00:02:48.239
And we would all be better off if there were
no unions and the workers had a direct relationship

00:02:48.239 --> 00:02:49.239
with the companies.

00:02:49.239 --> 00:02:52.989
Now, of course, we know that that's not the
case, and we know that as a result of the

00:02:52.989 --> 00:02:58.590
unions and being able to collectively bargain
in this way, the workers actually end up earning

00:02:58.590 --> 00:03:00.950
more on average, all else being equal.

00:03:00.950 --> 00:03:03.540
But that's not the story that Republicans
like to tell about unions.

00:03:03.540 --> 00:03:04.540
Speaker 2: You're going to pay dues.

00:03:04.540 --> 00:03:05.540
Speaker 3: You know, we don't force you.

00:03:05.540 --> 00:03:06.970
You're out of line.

00:03:06.970 --> 00:03:09.590
Speaker 2: No, don't tell me I'm on of line.

00:03:09.590 --> 00:03:10.590
Don't tell me I'm out of line.

00:03:10.590 --> 00:03:11.590
You can tell me.

00:03:11.590 --> 00:03:14.409
Speaker 3: I'm trying to frame you very misstating
your mouth.

00:03:14.409 --> 00:03:15.409
Yeah, because you don't.

00:03:15.409 --> 00:03:16.440
You're going to come out.

00:03:16.440 --> 00:03:17.440
Shut my mouth.

00:03:17.440 --> 00:03:18.459
Yes, I do it all day, tough guy.

00:03:18.459 --> 00:03:20.090
I'm not a physical holder.

00:03:20.090 --> 00:03:22.349
Speaker 2: But don't sit there and tell me
I'm out of line.

00:03:22.349 --> 00:03:23.569
Speaker 3: Senator, you made a statement.

00:03:23.569 --> 00:03:24.569
You asked the question.

00:03:24.569 --> 00:03:26.549
I didn't ask your question.

00:03:26.549 --> 00:03:28.900
You did it by the question.

00:03:28.900 --> 00:03:32.750
You asked the question about, Well, what do
you mean let him?

00:03:32.750 --> 00:03:33.750
And his.

00:03:33.750 --> 00:03:34.750
Speaker 2: Retort is.

00:03:34.750 --> 00:03:38.450
Speaker 3: Well, you may think it's rhetorical
to me like a question.

00:03:38.450 --> 00:03:39.670
Let him answer the question.

00:03:39.670 --> 00:03:41.290
Speaker 2: I'm not yield my time to him.

00:03:41.290 --> 00:03:42.610
So if you're going to let me keep my time,
that's fine.

00:03:42.610 --> 00:03:44.460
Speaker 3: You'll have your time.

00:03:44.460 --> 00:03:46.390
Let him you ask a question.

00:03:46.390 --> 00:03:50.280
He has a right to answer that as far as my
salary goes.

00:03:50.280 --> 00:03:51.280
My salary?

00:03:51.280 --> 00:03:52.280
If you follow me around, I walk.

00:03:52.280 --> 00:03:53.310
I actually look at this building.

00:03:53.310 --> 00:03:55.230
I bet you I work more hours than you do twice.

00:03:55.230 --> 00:03:56.269
But it is impossible.

00:03:56.269 --> 00:03:57.269
But that is.

00:03:57.269 --> 00:03:58.269
Speaker 2: That's true.

00:03:58.269 --> 00:03:59.269
So you know what?

00:03:59.269 --> 00:04:00.269
Hard work.

00:04:00.269 --> 00:04:04.700
Speaker 3: Second, when a public judge will
be doing it, we hold greedy CEOs like yourself

00:04:04.700 --> 00:04:05.700
accountable.

00:04:05.700 --> 00:04:07.700
Speaker 2: You call me a greedy C.
Speaker 3: Oh, yeah.

00:04:07.700 --> 00:04:08.700
You guys, you want to talk my salary?

00:04:08.700 --> 00:04:10.409
I'll tell you what do I make?

00:04:10.409 --> 00:04:11.780
What did you make when you were in your company?

00:04:11.780 --> 00:04:13.000
Speaker 2: I made my company.

00:04:13.000 --> 00:04:17.040
I kept my salary down to about 50,000 a year
because I'm now this.

00:04:17.040 --> 00:04:18.560
Speaker 1: We all know this game.

00:04:18.560 --> 00:04:19.560
This is so just.

00:04:19.560 --> 00:04:21.750
I kept my salary down.

00:04:21.750 --> 00:04:27.850
And of course, as CEO, Markwayne Mullin made
tens of millions of dollars towards his net

00:04:27.850 --> 00:04:31.160
worth from Non-salary compensation.

00:04:31.160 --> 00:04:32.350
Typical move.

00:04:32.350 --> 00:04:34.310
Maybe he thinks nobody else knows that.

00:04:34.310 --> 00:04:35.310
But we know that game.

00:04:35.310 --> 00:04:36.400
Speaker 2: Every penny into.

00:04:36.400 --> 00:04:37.400
Speaker 3: It.

00:04:37.400 --> 00:04:38.400
Okay.

00:04:38.400 --> 00:04:39.400
All right.

00:04:39.400 --> 00:04:40.400
You mean you hid money?

00:04:40.400 --> 00:04:41.400
Speaker 2: Oh.

00:04:41.400 --> 00:04:42.400
Speaker 3: Oh, oh.

00:04:42.400 --> 00:04:43.400
Hold on a second.

00:04:43.400 --> 00:04:45.810
Speaker 1: Okay, so there's a couple of different
things here that I think are important to

00:04:45.810 --> 00:04:48.270
mention.

00:04:48.270 --> 00:04:54.150
One of those things is the general idea that
Republicans want to put out there.

00:04:54.150 --> 00:04:57.270
And I don't even want to say that Republicans
believe they may not believe it.

00:04:57.270 --> 00:04:59.370
As we know, a lot of this stuff is just performance.

00:04:59.370 --> 00:05:06.940
The idea that they put out there is that rather
than a tool or an infrastructure that allows

00:05:06.940 --> 00:05:13.590
workers to organize and to pool their negotiating
strength in order to obtain better salary

00:05:13.590 --> 00:05:18.520
and benefits from a situation in which an
individual worker has disproportionately little

00:05:18.520 --> 00:05:19.520
power.

00:05:19.520 --> 00:05:20.520
Right.

00:05:20.520 --> 00:05:24.360
That's essentially what the union allows a
workers to do in order to get higher salary

00:05:24.360 --> 00:05:25.850
and better benefits.

00:05:25.850 --> 00:05:32.290
The union they frame as almost like a leech
that just collects dues forcibly, which, by

00:05:32.290 --> 00:05:37.210
the way, often is not the case that you're
forced into the union and to pay dues.

00:05:37.210 --> 00:05:40.111
In fact, sometimes it's the opposite, where
even if you refuse to pay the dues, the union

00:05:40.111 --> 00:05:41.111
still represents you.

00:05:41.111 --> 00:05:46.240
But that's a detail that depends on the union
and it takes away value.

00:05:46.240 --> 00:05:51.580
The idea is, Oh, the workers come out with
less because not only do they end up having

00:05:51.580 --> 00:05:55.389
to pay the dues, they don't even get a higher
salary or better benefits.

00:05:55.389 --> 00:05:56.490
We know that that's not the case.

00:05:56.490 --> 00:05:59.130
I mean, it's been studied endlessly and we
know that's not the case.

00:05:59.130 --> 00:06:00.639
So that's number one.

00:06:00.639 --> 00:06:07.440
And the number two, with all of these Republicans,
some personal hypocrisy is not far behind

00:06:07.440 --> 00:06:09.120
and it is not lost on me.

00:06:09.120 --> 00:06:10.180
And I hope it's not lost on you.

00:06:10.180 --> 00:06:19.050
The irony or the absurdity of Markwayne Mullin
calling the Teamsters president with a low

00:06:19.050 --> 00:06:25.900
six figure salary, the greedy CEO, when the
entire point of the Teamsters is to negotiate

00:06:25.900 --> 00:06:28.850
better pay and benefits for for tons of different
workers.

00:06:28.850 --> 00:06:35.120
When Markwayne Mullin's net worth and his
assets exploded by tens of millions of dollars,

00:06:35.120 --> 00:06:37.960
tens of millions of dollars over the last
few years.

00:06:37.960 --> 00:06:39.720
So they are pathetic.

00:06:39.720 --> 00:06:42.349
Yes, they are hypocrites, yes.

00:06:42.349 --> 00:06:45.050
But they also have to lie about unions.

00:06:45.050 --> 00:06:49.280
Now, I am not an unreasonable person when
it comes to unions.

00:06:49.280 --> 00:06:56.850
I recognize that there are lots of small businesses
where a union doesn't really make sense.

00:06:56.850 --> 00:06:58.740
And I am not someone who goes around as this.

00:06:58.740 --> 00:07:00.590
Every business should be unionized.

00:07:00.590 --> 00:07:02.110
That's just not my view.

00:07:02.110 --> 00:07:05.400
I've not come to believe that that's the best
thing.

00:07:05.400 --> 00:07:11.330
But when you're talking about these organizations
with tons of workers, electricians, electricians,

00:07:11.330 --> 00:07:16.389
union trade unions for particular companies,
UPS, etc..

00:07:16.389 --> 00:07:22.320
The dynamics of those companies and the sheer
scale of those companies are such that there

00:07:22.320 --> 00:07:28.190
has to be some mechanism for all of these
employees who have shared interests to be

00:07:28.190 --> 00:07:30.870
able to pool their power.

00:07:30.870 --> 00:07:36.819
Because remember, when there are 30,000, 60,000
employees or whatever the case may be, one

00:07:36.819 --> 00:07:41.540
person, if forced to negotiate individually,
has relatively little power because they can

00:07:41.540 --> 00:07:47.470
just be excluded or pushed to the side or
their influence minimized when the negotiation

00:07:47.470 --> 00:07:48.569
is happening.

00:07:48.569 --> 00:07:53.889
Collectively, that is a very different situation
and it puts large companies in the position

00:07:53.889 --> 00:07:57.490
of having to really come to the table, not
necessarily in good faith, but at least willing

00:07:57.490 --> 00:07:58.909
to negotiate in a real way.

00:07:58.909 --> 00:08:03.580
So very interesting exchange and shows the
continued hostility towards labor from the

00:08:03.580 --> 00:08:04.349
modern Republican Party.

